# id: BM21-network-in-build
# blinds: the property that no lane touches the network (DESIGN-B1 section 9.2)
# covering-lane: cpp-ci
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-lane-set
# expect-control: alive
#
# BM21 IS ITS OWN CONTROL, and that is why it is the one mutant that carries a
# control-lane. The induced failure DESIGN-B1 names for it is bidirectional in
# its own wording -- "it must pass with networking available and fail under
# isolation" -- so a red under isolation only means something once the same
# patched tree has been seen to go green with a network. Without that half, a
# patch that simply broke the build would look exactly like a caught mutant.
#
# The SOURCE_SUBDIR names a directory that does not exist, so
# FetchContent_MakeAvailable populates without add_subdirectory. That is not a
# trick to make the mutant pass: it is what keeps the mutant testing the
# NETWORK property rather than colliding with the vendored gtest targets and
# failing for an unrelated reason in both directions.
--- a/engine-cpp/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-24 16:39:42
+++ b/engine-cpp/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-24 16:39:42
@@ -46,3 +46,16 @@
 target_compile_options(gtest_main PRIVATE -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti)
 target_compile_definitions(gtest      PUBLIC GTEST_HAS_EXCEPTIONS=0 GTEST_HAS_RTTI=0)
 target_compile_definitions(gtest_main PUBLIC GTEST_HAS_EXCEPTIONS=0 GTEST_HAS_RTTI=0)
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------- BM21 (injected defect)
+# The realistic mistake: someone decides vendoring is a nuisance and reaches
+# for FetchContent. It works on their machine, and on any machine with a
+# network, which is every machine anyone tries it on.
+include(FetchContent)
+FetchContent_Declare(
+  bm21_gtest
+  GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/google/googletest.git
+  GIT_TAG        52eb8108c5bdec04579160ae17225d66034bd723
+  SOURCE_SUBDIR  rift-bm21-no-such-dir
+)
+FetchContent_MakeAvailable(bm21_gtest)
